Subject: [PATCH] camera S2: a GPU ISP prototype on Adreno 640 through OpenGL ES 3.0 (cam_gpu=1, NEON fallback)

Base: work/hkd-ws20/userland (the root-cam2 line; src/camera_isp.c sha256 e8b35f8b0e98...,
src/camera_device.c b3d92562bb61..., src/camera_isp.h 63187a8d65a1..., build.sh e9fcd752c9f3...).
Everything else in that tree is unchanged. The paths are the repository's: `patch -p1` at the
repository root, `patch -p2` inside the userland tree itself (as 0066 does: `a/userland/...`).

What it adds:
- src/camera_isp_gpu.c: the GPU ISP. OpenGL ES 3.0 off-screen through Mesa's OSMesa, the same
  library and interface the window server draws with (src/gl_osmesa.c, lib/libOSMesa.8.dylib in
  the image), only dlopen()ed: libhakios.media.dylib gains no load-time dependency (otool -L: the
  system frameworks alone), and a mediaserverd whose image has no libOSMesa keeps the processor's
  path. The context is made on the first frame that asks for it; a failure of any step (no library,
  no context, a shader the driver will not take) leaves isp->gpu false for the rest of the run and
  camera_isp.c makes the picture as before -- the fallback the boot argument promises.
- src/camera_isp_gpu_shaders.h: the two passes. Pass 1: RAW10's four-in-five-bytes packing out of
  an R8UI texture, black 64 off, the 17x13 lens-shading grid bilinear per 2x2 cell (as
  camera_isp.c's lsc_at()), Malvar-He-Cutler 5x5 demosaic, the white balance's and AE's gains with
  the colour matrix (pipe_init()'s rows) and the 4096-step sRGB/tone table, to RGBA8UI. Pass 2:
  BT.601 in 1/65536ths with nv12_pair()'s rounding, an optional 3x3 bilateral on Y (sigma in
  codes), the stills' unsharp mask (threshold 2, +-24), and the 2x2 block sums for U and V; MRT to
  R8UI (Y) and RG8UI (UV), one draw. The UV target is picture-sized: each 2x2 block's four pixels
  write the block's pair, and the readback keeps one column in two (cam_gpu_nr= is the sigma).
- camera_isp.c: isp_set_gpu()/isp_gpu_start(), and the hook in isp_render_nv12() and
  isp_render_nv12_half() for a whole picture (first_row 0, end_row >= height), false -> the
  processor's path. camera_device.c: cam_gpu=1 and cam_gpu_nr=F parsed in load_fast(), one log
  line ("the GPU ISP: on/off"), and render_piece() gives the whole frame to the first piece when
  the GPU is on (the other pieces have nothing to do; stills and BGRA keep the processor's path).
- tools/cam_gpu_bench.c (built to /redwood/bin/cam_gpu_bench, ad-hoc signed when ldid is there),
  tools/cam_gpu_scene.h (a scene made up as an IMX582's RAW10: 1920x1080, stride 2400, black 64,
  bayer 0), tools/cam_gpu_ref.h (the C reference of the shaders' arithmetic) and
  tools/cam_gpu_host.c (the same shaders through Mesa's EGL on the host, held to the C reference
  and to isp_render_nv12()).

Host protocol (2026-11-02, no phone, no gunwest; Mesa 22.3.6 llvmpipe, EGL surfaceless, GL ES 3.2;
frame 1920x1080 made up as above; the tuning is fw-poco-x3-pro/camera-imx582.isp):
- shaders against the C reference of their own arithmetic: Y 50.18 dB, UV 56.02 dB (detail),
  50.29/56.70 (smooth), 51.04/56.11 (sigma 1.5, unsharp 0.3), 50.98/56.63 (420v).
- shaders against isp_render_nv12(): Y 26.93 dB, UV 34.96 dB (detail); 36.76/41.87 (smooth);
  28.23/36.07 (420v). Below the plan's 38 dB: isp_render_nv12() is the preview's one-pixel-per-2x2-
  cell picture (a quarter of the demosaic, upscaled), the shader is a full-resolution Malvar one;
  the C reference differs from isp_render_nv12() by the same 26.87/36.35 dB, so the gap is the
  algorithm, not the GLSL. A like-for-like 38 dB needs the cell path in the shader or a full-
  resolution processor reference.
- 29-43 ms a frame on llvmpipe (a host rasterizer; NOT the Adreno's time -- no phone run).
- There is no dump of a real IMX582 RAW10 frame in artifacts/vayu (only .raw files that are not
  frames: sel.tar.raw, vayu_ssc.c.raw, XBLCore.raw), so the frames above are made up; --raw takes
  one when there is one.

Not done: IOSurface memory as the textures and the targets (hakios_gl_surface_texture()'s way), so
the frame's bytes are copied up (2.6 MB) and the picture down (Y in place, UV through a
picture-sized buffer); that is the next step. No phone run: the arm64 side is compile- and
link-checked only (media dylib and bench), the shaders are checked on the host.

